{"reason": "The validation log-loss plateaued at 0.4356, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis that the frozen backbone is a bottleneck. The historical experiment successfully unfroze DeBERTa with a warm-up phase and differential learning rates, achieving a validation log-loss of 0.3803. However, that experiment also used a lower max_length (128), increased early stopping patience, and removed features to reduce memory. This refinement will adapt those successful strategies to the current code: unfreeze the backbone after a warm-up, reduce max_length to 128 (which also reduces GPU memory usage), increase patience to 6 to allow more epochs for fine-tuning, and remove the engineered features to simplify the model and avoid unnecessary computations. The changes are focused on 'training_evaluation' and 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' modules. The model architecture remains unchanged, so 'model_design' is not modified.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove engineered features (char/word counts, n-grams, TF-IDF) and keep only the text for the transformer model. WHY: The initial plan's root cause analysis indicates the frozen backbone is the bottleneck; engineered features are not needed and may add noise. The historical successful experiment did not use features and achieved better performance. HOW: In the data loading section, skip all feature engineering steps. Directly use raw text from train_df and test_df for tokenization. Keep the label encoder and StratifiedKFold split. The train and val loaders will only use tokenized text, no extra feature vectors. Interface constraints: preserve variable names like train_texts, val_texts, test_texts, train_labels_encoded, etc.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Unfreeze the DeBERTa backbone after 5 epochs of warm-up with frozen backbone; (2) Implement a differential learning rate (1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head) with a cosine scheduler; (3) Increase early stopping patience from 4 to 6; (4) Reduce max_length from 256 to 128 to save memory. WHY: The initial plan attributes the plateau to frozen backbone; unfreezing with warm-up and differential LR allows gentle fine-tuning. Historical experiment with these settings (plus patience=6, max_length=128) achieved val log-loss 0.3803. Increasing patience gives more epochs for fine-tuning, and reducing max_length lowers GPU memory usage without major performance loss for this sentence-level task. HOW: In the training loop, after epoch 5, set requires_grad=True for backbone parameters. Modify optimizer_grouped_parameters to include two groups: backbone (lr=1e-6) and head (lr=2e-5). Add a cosine scheduler with warm-up (e.g., total steps = num_epochs * len(train_loader), warmup steps = 5 * len(train_loader)). Change patience to 6. Set MAX_SEQ_LENGTH = 128 in the model configuration and tokenizer calls. Keep all other training parameters (batch_size=8, AdamW, gradient clipping, mixed precision) unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The validation log-loss plateaued at 0.4356, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis that the frozen backbone is a bottleneck. The historical experiment successfully unfroze DeBERTa with a warm-up phase and differential learning rates, achieving a validation log-loss of 0.3803. However, that experiment also used a lower max_length (128), increased early stopping patience, and removed features to reduce memory. This refinement will adapt those successful strategies to the current code: unfreeze the backbone after a warm-up, reduce max_length to 128 (which also reduces GPU memory usage), increase patience to 6 to allow more epochs for fine-tuning, and remove the engineered features to simplify the model and avoid unnecessary computations. The changes are focused on 'training_evaluation' and 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' modules. The model architecture remains unchanged, so 'model_design' is not modified.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove engineered features (char/word counts, n-grams, TF-IDF) and keep only the text for the transformer model. WHY: The initial plan's root cause analysis indicates the frozen backbone is the bottleneck; engineered features are not needed and may add noise. The historical successful experiment did not use features and achieved better performance. HOW: In the data loading section, skip all feature engineering steps. Directly use raw text from train_df and test_df for tokenization. Keep the label encoder and StratifiedKFold split. The train and val loaders will only use tokenized text, no extra feature vectors. Interface constraints: preserve variable names like train_texts, val_texts, test_texts, train_labels_encoded, etc.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Unfreeze the DeBERTa backbone after 5 epochs of warm-up with frozen backbone; (2) Implement a differential learning rate (1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head) with a cosine scheduler; (3) Increase early stopping patience from 4 to 6; (4) Reduce max_length from 256 to 128 to save memory. WHY: The initial plan attributes the plateau to frozen backbone; unfreezing with warm-up and differential LR allows gentle fine-tuning. Historical experiment with these settings (plus patience=6, max_length=128) achieved val log-loss 0.3803. Increasing patience gives more epochs for fine-tuning, and reducing max_length lowers GPU memory usage without major performance loss for this sentence-level task. HOW: In the training loop, after epoch 5, set requires_grad=True for backbone parameters. Modify optimizer_grouped_parameters to include two groups: backbone (lr=1e-6) and head (lr=2e-5). Add a cosine scheduler with warm-up (e.g., total steps = num_epochs * len(train_loader), warmup steps = 5 * len(train_loader)). Change patience to 6. Set MAX_SEQ_LENGTH = 128 in the model configuration and tokenizer calls. Keep all other training parameters (batch_size=8, AdamW, gradient clipping, mixed precision) unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan identified root causes: frozen backbone and 128-token max_length limiting performance. Historical successful experiments showed that unfreezing the backbone with warm-up and differential learning rates (1e-6 backbone, 2e-5 head) achieved 0.3803 val log-loss, while a failed experiment plateaued at 0.4356 with frozen backbone. The current execution plateaued at 0.4410, confirming the need for fine-tuning. The initial plan's changes (model upgrade, seq_length increase, staged training) are sound but must be refined with lessons from history: using a single max_length (256), removing engineered features (they add noise), and implementing gentle fine-tuning with differential LR and cosine schedule. Thus, modifications target 'model_design' (unfreeze backbone, differential LR), 'training_evaluation' (cosine schedule, warm-up, patience increase), and 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' (remove features, increase seq_length).", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (char/word counts, n-grams, TF-IDF) and keep only raw text for tokenization. Increase max_length from 128 to 256. WHY: The initial plan's root cause analysis shows frozen backbone as main bottleneck; engineered features are unnecessary and add noise. Historical successful experiment used no features and achieved better performance. HOW: In DATA LOADING section, skip all feature engineering steps. Directly use train_df['text'] and test_df['text'] for tokenization with max_length=256. Keep label encoder and StratifiedKFold split. Interface constraints: preserve variable names train_texts, val_texts, test_texts, train_labels_encoded, etc.", "model_design": "WHAT: Unfreeze the DeBERTa backbone with differential learning rates (1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head). Keep the same model architecture (SpookyAuthorClassifier) but allow backbone fine-tuning after warm-up. WHY: The initial plan diagnoses frozen backbone as bottleneck; unfreezing with differential LR adapts pretrained representations to domain while preventing catastrophic forgetting. Historical successful experiment validated this approach achieving 0.3803 val log-loss. HOW: After warm-up epochs, set requires_grad=True for backbone. Modify optimizer to have two parameter groups: backbone (lr=1e-6) and head (lr=2e-5). Preserve all other model components (MultiScaleConvBlock, AttentionPooling, classifier).", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement two-stage training: first 5 epochs freeze backbone (only train head), then unfreeze backbone and apply cosine learning rate schedule with warm-up (10% of steps). Increase early stopping patience to 6. Use gradient accumulation steps=4 with effective batch size=8. WHY: Staged training prevents catastrophic forgetting; cosine schedule with warmup improves convergence; historical experiment with these settings (patience=6) achieved 0.3803. Gradient accumulation maintains stable gradients despite larger max_length. HOW: In TRAINING AND EVALUATION section, after epoch 5, unfreeze backbone and reinitialize optimizer with two groups and cosine scheduler. Set patience=6. Use GradScaler for mixed precision. Keep CombinedLoss, AdamW optimizer, and early stopping logic unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identified root causes: frozen backbone and 128-token max_length limiting performance. Historical successful experiments showed that unfreezing the backbone with warm-up and differential learning rates (1e-6 backbone, 2e-5 head) achieved 0.3803 val log-loss, while a failed experiment plateaued at 0.4356 with frozen backbone. The current execution plateaued at 0.4410, confirming the need for fine-tuning. The initial plan's changes (model upgrade, seq_length increase, staged training) are sound but must be refined with lessons from history: using a single max_length (256), removing engineered features (they add noise), and implementing gentle fine-tuning with differential LR and cosine schedule. Thus, modifications target 'model_design' (unfreeze backbone, differential LR), 'training_evaluation' (cosine schedule, warm-up, patience increase), and 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' (remove features, increase seq_length).",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (char/word counts, n-grams, TF-IDF) and keep only raw text for tokenization. Increase max_length from 128 to 256. WHY: The initial plan's root cause analysis shows frozen backbone as main bottleneck; engineered features are unnecessary and add noise. Historical successful experiment used no features and achieved better performance. HOW: In DATA LOADING section, skip all feature engineering steps. Directly use train_df['text'] and test_df['text'] for tokenization with max_length=256. Keep label encoder and StratifiedKFold split. Interface constraints: preserve variable names train_texts, val_texts, test_texts, train_labels_encoded, etc.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Unfreeze the DeBERTa backbone with differential learning rates (1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head). Keep the same model architecture (SpookyAuthorClassifier) but allow backbone fine-tuning after warm-up. WHY: The initial plan diagnoses frozen backbone as bottleneck; unfreezing with differential LR adapts pretrained representations to domain while preventing catastrophic forgetting. Historical successful experiment validated this approach achieving 0.3803 val log-loss. HOW: After warm-up epochs, set requires_grad=True for backbone. Modify optimizer to have two parameter groups: backbone (lr=1e-6) and head (lr=2e-5). Preserve all other model components (MultiScaleConvBlock, AttentionPooling, classifier).",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement two-stage training: first 5 epochs freeze backbone (only train head), then unfreeze backbone and apply cosine learning rate schedule with warm-up (10% of steps). Increase early stopping patience to 6. Use gradient accumulation steps=4 with effective batch size=8. WHY: Staged training prevents catastrophic forgetting; cosine schedule with warmup improves convergence; historical experiment with these settings (patience=6) achieved 0.3803. Gradient accumulation maintains stable gradients despite larger max_length. HOW: In TRAINING AND EVALUATION section, after epoch 5, unfreeze backbone and reinitialize optimizer with two groups and cosine scheduler. Set patience=6. Use GradScaler for mixed precision. Keep CombinedLoss, AdamW optimizer, and early stopping logic unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan's pseudo-labeling strategy is sound but requires modifications to avoid overfitting and computational overhead. Historical successful experiments show that unfreezing the backbone with differential learning rates and a warm-up phase, combined with a larger training set (using all training data), significantly improved performance (val log-loss 0.3803). The failed experiment removed features but still used a frozen backbone for too long, which plateaued. The current execution shows validation log-loss plateauing at 0.4356 with a frozen backbone and only 80% of training data. The refined plan will: (1) simplify pseudo-labeling by using a single model trained on all training data (instead of 5-fold cross-validation) to generate pseudo-labels, reducing computational cost; (2) unfreeze the backbone after a warm-up phase with differential learning rates to improve representation learning; (3) remove engineered features to simplify the model and reduce memory, as historical success showed; (4) reduce max_length to 128 to save memory and allow larger effective batch size; (5) increase early stopping patience to 6. These modifications address the representation bottleneck and leverage historical successes.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (char/word counts, n-grams, TF-IDF, etc.) and only use raw text for the transformer model. WHY: Historical successful experiments without engineered features achieved better performance (0.3803 val log-loss) compared to current plateau (0.4356). Engineered features add noise and require significant preprocessing, and the transformer already learns rich representations from text. HOW: In the data processing section, skip all feature engineering steps. Directly tokenize raw text from train_df and test_df. Keep the label encoder and StratifiedKFold split (but use full training data for final model). Interface constraints: preserve variable names like train_texts, val_texts, test_texts, train_labels_encoded, etc. The feature extraction functions and scaler can be removed entirely.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Implement pseudo-labeling: train a model on all training data (full 17621 samples) with a mini validation set (500 samples) for early stopping, then use this model to predict test set probabilities, select top 500 high-confidence test samples as pseudo-labels, and retrain on augmented data (full train + pseudo-labeled test). (2) Unfreeze the DeBERTa backbone after a warm-up of 5 epochs with frozen backbone, then use differential learning rates (1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head) with cosine scheduler and warm-up. (3) Reduce max_length from 256 to 128 to save GPU memory and speed up training. (4) Increase early stopping patience from 4 to 6 to allow more time for fine-tuning. WHY: The initial plan attributes the plateau (0.4356) to frozen backbone and limited training data (80% split). Pseudo-labeling increases effective training size, unfreezing with warm-up and differential LR allows gentle fine-tuning, reducing max_length lowers memory, and increasing patience allows more epochs for convergence. Historical success with these settings achieved 0.3803 val log-loss. HOW: In the training loop, after epoch 5 set requires_grad=True for backbone parameters. Modify optimizer_grouped_parameters to include two groups: backbone (lr=1e-6, weight_decay=0.01) and head (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01). Add a cosine scheduler with warm-up (warmup_steps=5*train_loader_steps, total_steps=num_epochs*train_loader_steps). Set MAX_SEQ_LENGTH=128. Change patience=6. Keep batch_size=8, AdamW, gradient clipping=1.0, mixed precision (AMP). Add pseudo-labeling phase before final training: train initial model on full training data with mini validation, predict test probabilities, select top 500 with max probability>0.9, add them to training set with pseudo-labels, then train final model on augmented set with same early stopping."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's pseudo-labeling strategy is sound but requires modifications to avoid overfitting and computational overhead. Historical successful experiments show that unfreezing the backbone with differential learning rates and a warm-up phase, combined with a larger training set (using all training data), significantly improved performance (val log-loss 0.3803). The failed experiment removed features but still used a frozen backbone for too long, which plateaued. The current execution shows validation log-loss plateauing at 0.4356 with a frozen backbone and only 80% of training data. The refined plan will: (1) simplify pseudo-labeling by using a single model trained on all training data (instead of 5-fold cross-validation) to generate pseudo-labels, reducing computational cost; (2) unfreeze the backbone after a warm-up phase with differential learning rates to improve representation learning; (3) remove engineered features to simplify the model and reduce memory, as historical success showed; (4) reduce max_length to 128 to save memory and allow larger effective batch size; (5) increase early stopping patience to 6. These modifications address the representation bottleneck and leverage historical successes.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (char/word counts, n-grams, TF-IDF, etc.) and only use raw text for the transformer model. WHY: Historical successful experiments without engineered features achieved better performance (0.3803 val log-loss) compared to current plateau (0.4356). Engineered features add noise and require significant preprocessing, and the transformer already learns rich representations from text. HOW: In the data processing section, skip all feature engineering steps. Directly tokenize raw text from train_df and test_df. Keep the label encoder and StratifiedKFold split (but use full training data for final model). Interface constraints: preserve variable names like train_texts, val_texts, test_texts, train_labels_encoded, etc. The feature extraction functions and scaler can be removed entirely.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Implement pseudo-labeling: train a model on all training data (full 17621 samples) with a mini validation set (500 samples) for early stopping, then use this model to predict test set probabilities, select top 500 high-confidence test samples as pseudo-labels, and retrain on augmented data (full train + pseudo-labeled test). (2) Unfreeze the DeBERTa backbone after a warm-up of 5 epochs with frozen backbone, then use differential learning rates (1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head) with cosine scheduler and warm-up. (3) Reduce max_length from 256 to 128 to save GPU memory and speed up training. (4) Increase early stopping patience from 4 to 6 to allow more time for fine-tuning. WHY: The initial plan attributes the plateau (0.4356) to frozen backbone and limited training data (80% split). Pseudo-labeling increases effective training size, unfreezing with warm-up and differential LR allows gentle fine-tuning, reducing max_length lowers memory, and increasing patience allows more epochs for convergence. Historical success with these settings achieved 0.3803 val log-loss. HOW: In the training loop, after epoch 5 set requires_grad=True for backbone parameters. Modify optimizer_grouped_parameters to include two groups: backbone (lr=1e-6, weight_decay=0.01) and head (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01). Add a cosine scheduler with warm-up (warmup_steps=5*train_loader_steps, total_steps=num_epochs*train_loader_steps). Set MAX_SEQ_LENGTH=128. Change patience=6. Keep batch_size=8, AdamW, gradient clipping=1.0, mixed precision (AMP). Add pseudo-labeling phase before final training: train initial model on full training data with mini validation, predict test probabilities, select top 500 with max probability>0.9, add them to training set with pseudo-labels, then train final model on augmented set with same early stopping."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan proposed multi-scale feature extraction and validation feature stacking. The historical failed experiment showed that freezing the backbone leads to plateaus, while successful experiments unfroze with warm-up and achieved better performance. The execution results show validation log-loss plateauing around 0.30 with early stopping at epoch 8. To improve, I will follow the successful historical pattern: remove engineered features, unfreeze backbone with warm-up and differential learning rates, use a multi-scale conv head to capture local patterns, and keep stacking. This refines the initial plan by focusing on the most impactful changes suggested by history.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (basic, POS, author vocabulary, emotion, n-grams) and use only raw text for the transformer. WHY: Historical successful experiments without features achieved better validation log-loss (0.3803) while the failed experiment with frozen backbone and features plateaued. The transformer alone is sufficient; features add noise and computational overhead. HOW: In data processing, only load the raw text from CSV files. Remove all feature extraction functions and their calls. Keep label encoding for author column. Use StratifiedKFold or train_test_split as before. Interface constraints: maintain variables X_train_texts, X_val_texts, y_train, y_val as arrays of raw text and labels.", "model_design": "WHAT: Add multi-scale feature extraction after the base model. After the base model outputs hidden states, apply parallel 1D convolutions with kernel sizes [2,3,5,7] followed by adaptive max pooling, then concatenate with [CLS] token. Use a learned attention pooling instead of just [CLS]. Replace the simple classifier with a two-layer MLP (512-256-3) with LayerNorm and GELU. WHY: The initial plan correctly identifies that [CLS] alone misses local patterns. Multi-scale convs capture n-gram-like features. Historical successful experiments used similar conv blocks and achieved better results. HOW: Modify AuthorClassifier to include a MultiScaleConvBlock module with nn.Conv1d layers, adaptivemaxpool, and a gated aggregation. The forward pass should use the base model's hidden states for both [CLS] and conv features. Interface constraints: model must accept input_ids, attention_mask, labels and return dict with loss and logits.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Unfreeze the backbone after 5 epochs of warm-up (initially frozen). (2) Use differential learning rates: 1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head. (3) Increase early stopping patience from 5 to 6. (4) Add validation feature stacking: after each epoch, save out-of-fold predictions from best model, then train a lightweight XGBoost meta-classifier on concatenated transformer probs + manual features (from previous data processing, but kept minimal). WHY: Historical successful experiments unfroze backbone with warm-up and reached lower log-loss (0.3803 vs current 0.30 plateau). Combined stacking with manual features (as in initial plan) can further reduce overfitting. HOW: In training loop, first 5 epochs keep backbone params requires_grad=False, then set True. Use two parameter groups in optimizer. Implement a separate stacking pipeline after best model selection. Interface constraints: keep same data loaders, but after best model, generate val_preds and use them plus original X_val_texts to create meta-features for stacking."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan proposed multi-scale feature extraction and validation feature stacking. The historical failed experiment showed that freezing the backbone leads to plateaus, while successful experiments unfroze with warm-up and achieved better performance. The execution results show validation log-loss plateauing around 0.30 with early stopping at epoch 8. To improve, I will follow the successful historical pattern: remove engineered features, unfreeze backbone with warm-up and differential learning rates, use a multi-scale conv head to capture local patterns, and keep stacking. This refines the initial plan by focusing on the most impactful changes suggested by history.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (basic, POS, author vocabulary, emotion, n-grams) and use only raw text for the transformer. WHY: Historical successful experiments without features achieved better validation log-loss (0.3803) while the failed experiment with frozen backbone and features plateaued. The transformer alone is sufficient; features add noise and computational overhead. HOW: In data processing, only load the raw text from CSV files. Remove all feature extraction functions and their calls. Keep label encoding for author column. Use StratifiedKFold or train_test_split as before. Interface constraints: maintain variables X_train_texts, X_val_texts, y_train, y_val as arrays of raw text and labels.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Add multi-scale feature extraction after the base model. After the base model outputs hidden states, apply parallel 1D convolutions with kernel sizes [2,3,5,7] followed by adaptive max pooling, then concatenate with [CLS] token. Use a learned attention pooling instead of just [CLS]. Replace the simple classifier with a two-layer MLP (512-256-3) with LayerNorm and GELU. WHY: The initial plan correctly identifies that [CLS] alone misses local patterns. Multi-scale convs capture n-gram-like features. Historical successful experiments used similar conv blocks and achieved better results. HOW: Modify AuthorClassifier to include a MultiScaleConvBlock module with nn.Conv1d layers, adaptivemaxpool, and a gated aggregation. The forward pass should use the base model's hidden states for both [CLS] and conv features. Interface constraints: model must accept input_ids, attention_mask, labels and return dict with loss and logits.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Unfreeze the backbone after 5 epochs of warm-up (initially frozen). (2) Use differential learning rates: 1e-6 for backbone, 2e-5 for head. (3) Increase early stopping patience from 5 to 6. (4) Add validation feature stacking: after each epoch, save out-of-fold predictions from best model, then train a lightweight XGBoost meta-classifier on concatenated transformer probs + manual features (from previous data processing, but kept minimal). WHY: Historical successful experiments unfroze backbone with warm-up and reached lower log-loss (0.3803 vs current 0.30 plateau). Combined stacking with manual features (as in initial plan) can further reduce overfitting. HOW: In training loop, first 5 epochs keep backbone params requires_grad=False, then set True. Use two parameter groups in optimizer. Implement a separate stacking pipeline after best model selection. Interface constraints: keep same data loaders, but after best model, generate val_preds and use them plus original X_val_texts to create meta-features for stacking."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows severe overfitting (train loss drops to 0.08 while val loss increases after epoch 2). The validation log loss of 0.415 is still high. Similar historical experiments show that progressive unfreezing with a warm-up phase and lower learning rates achieved better validation loss (0.380). Therefore, I focus on training_evaluation to implement progressive unfreezing, cosine annealing scheduler, and SWA as proposed in the initial plan. Additionally, I modify data_processing_and_feature_engineering based on a successful historical experiment that removed engineered features to reduce overfitting and simplify the model. The model_design is kept unchanged except for removing the feature concatenation.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (basic stats, punctuation, readability, POS, sentiment, vocabulary, char n-grams, word TF-IDF) and use only raw text for the transformer model. WHY: The initial plan identifies overfitting due to training too aggressively, and historical experiments show that simplified models without engineered features achieved better validation loss (0.380) by focusing on transformer fine-tuning. The current feature set may introduce noise and increase memory usage. HOW: In data loading section, skip all feature engineering functions and transformations. Directly use cleaned text for tokenization. Remove feature scaling and saving of feature matrices. Preserve label encoder and StratifiedKFold split. Update train/val/test loaders to only provide tokenized text. Adjust model accordingly (remove engineered_features input).", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Implement progressive unfreezing: freeze all DeBERTa layers initially, train only classifier for 2 epochs; then unfreeze last 6 layers for 3 epochs; then unfreeze last 12 layers for 3 epochs; then fully unfreeze for remaining epochs. (2) Replace linear scheduler with cosine annealing with warm restarts (T_0=5, T_mult=2). (3) Add Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) that averages weights over the last 5 epochs, with an additional 3 epochs of training at constant high LR (1e-5 for classifier, 5e-6 for backbone) before averaging. (4) Increase early stopping patience to 6. (5) Remove engineered features from model forward (since they are removed). WHY: Progressive unfreezing establishes a curriculum that prevents early overfitting; cosine annealing helps escape local minima; SWA smoothes the loss landscape for better generalization. This directly addresses the overfitting pattern observed (train loss << val loss) and builds on historical success. The patience increase allows more epochs for fine-tuning. HOW: In training loop, add epoch-based unfreezing logic with requires_grad. Replace scheduler with torch.optim.lr_scheduler.CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts(optimizer, T_0=5, T_mult=2). After main loop, run 3 additional epochs with optimizer.lr=1e-5 (classifier) and set backbone lr=5e-6, collect weights every step for SWA averaging (use torch.optim.swa_utils.AveragedModel). Update model forward to not accept engineered_features. Keep batch_size=8, AdamW, mixed precision, gradient accumulation=2, max_length=512. Ensure submission file saved to ./submission/submission.csv."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows severe overfitting (train loss drops to 0.08 while val loss increases after epoch 2). The validation log loss of 0.415 is still high. Similar historical experiments show that progressive unfreezing with a warm-up phase and lower learning rates achieved better validation loss (0.380). Therefore, I focus on training_evaluation to implement progressive unfreezing, cosine annealing scheduler, and SWA as proposed in the initial plan. Additionally, I modify data_processing_and_feature_engineering based on a successful historical experiment that removed engineered features to reduce overfitting and simplify the model. The model_design is kept unchanged except for removing the feature concatenation.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Remove all engineered features (basic stats, punctuation, readability, POS, sentiment, vocabulary, char n-grams, word TF-IDF) and use only raw text for the transformer model. WHY: The initial plan identifies overfitting due to training too aggressively, and historical experiments show that simplified models without engineered features achieved better validation loss (0.380) by focusing on transformer fine-tuning. The current feature set may introduce noise and increase memory usage. HOW: In data loading section, skip all feature engineering functions and transformations. Directly use cleaned text for tokenization. Remove feature scaling and saving of feature matrices. Preserve label encoder and StratifiedKFold split. Update train/val/test loaders to only provide tokenized text. Adjust model accordingly (remove engineered_features input).",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: (1) Implement progressive unfreezing: freeze all DeBERTa layers initially, train only classifier for 2 epochs; then unfreeze last 6 layers for 3 epochs; then unfreeze last 12 layers for 3 epochs; then fully unfreeze for remaining epochs. (2) Replace linear scheduler with cosine annealing with warm restarts (T_0=5, T_mult=2). (3) Add Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) that averages weights over the last 5 epochs, with an additional 3 epochs of training at constant high LR (1e-5 for classifier, 5e-6 for backbone) before averaging. (4) Increase early stopping patience to 6. (5) Remove engineered features from model forward (since they are removed). WHY: Progressive unfreezing establishes a curriculum that prevents early overfitting; cosine annealing helps escape local minima; SWA smoothes the loss landscape for better generalization. This directly addresses the overfitting pattern observed (train loss << val loss) and builds on historical success. The patience increase allows more epochs for fine-tuning. HOW: In training loop, add epoch-based unfreezing logic with requires_grad. Replace scheduler with torch.optim.lr_scheduler.CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts(optimizer, T_0=5, T_mult=2). After main loop, run 3 additional epochs with optimizer.lr=1e-5 (classifier) and set backbone lr=5e-6, collect weights every step for SWA averaging (use torch.optim.swa_utils.AveragedModel). Update model forward to not accept engineered_features. Keep batch_size=8, AdamW, mixed precision, gradient accumulation=2, max_length=512. Ensure submission file saved to ./submission/submission.csv."
